/** Standalone batch AF -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output.
 *
 * uva-907-winterim-backpacking-trip: binary search the minimum feasible "max day distance", checked
 *   via the same greedy group-count feasibility test as uva-714's copying-books (fill each day up to
 *   the candidate cap, count how many days that needs, must be <= K+1).
 * uva-913-joana-and-the-odd-numbers: row k (1-indexed) holds 2k-1 odd numbers, with (k-1)^2 odd
 *   numbers used in all prior rows; the last 3 numbers in a row of size N are the ((k-1)^2+N-2)-th
 *   through ((k-1)^2+N)-th odd numbers overall (value = 2*index-1 each), giving a closed-form sum of
 *   6*((k-1)^2+N)-9. Verified against the problem statement's own N=55 example and two smaller rows by
 *   hand.
 * uva-929-number-maze: Dijkstra via a bucket queue (dial's algorithm), since cell costs are always
 *   0-9 -- far more efficient than a binary heap at the ~10^6-cell maximum grid size.
 * uva-941-permutations: factorial-number-system ranking generalized to MULTISETS (repeated letters
 *   divide out their own repeat-count factorials from the permutation-count formula at each digit
 *   position), verified against both the problem's own examples and an explicit repeated-letter case.
 * uva-967-circular: sieve to 10^6 once, then for every prime check all cyclic rotations (each parsed
 *   as a plain integer, so a rotation landing on a leading zero is handled correctly via ordinary
 *   numeric parsing) are also prime; a prefix-sum array then answers each range query in O(1).
 * uva-1200-a-dp-problem: parse each side's terms (regex matching optional-sign digit-runs optionally
 *   followed by 'x'), combine into one coefficient-of-x and one constant per side; if the combined
 *   coefficient of x is zero, the equation is IDENTITY (constant also zero) or IMPOSSIBLE. The real
 *   bug caught here: the answer is FLOOR(x), not the exact (generally non-integer) algebraic solution
 *   -- the misleading slug name ("a dp problem") obscured that this is a linear-equation solver, and
 *   the real problem statement (fetched directly, since the extracted title didn't match) confirmed
 *   the floor requirement.
 * uva-1207-agtc: standard Levenshtein edit-distance DP (insertion/deletion cost 1, substitution cost 0
 *   or 1 depending on whether the characters already match). The real bug caught here: each dataset is
 *   TWO LINES, each containing "<length> <string>" together (not four separate lines as a naive read
 *   of "input consists of ... their lengths, one in each line" might suggest) -- confirmed directly
 *   against the real sample's raw byte layout.
 */
